WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.575
A customer calls the payments help line.

2
00:00:05.575 --> 00:00:07.300
Why is my balance 20.00?

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:13.769
Support opens the accounts table: one row for her account, acct-17, and one number, 20.00.

4
00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:15.207
That's all there is.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.054
Twenty.

6
00:00:16.054 --> 00:00:17.455
But why twenty?

7
00:00:17.455 --> 00:00:19.324
The table can't say.

8
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.621
Now think of your own bank app.

9
00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:24.719
The balance sits at the top, and under it, a list.

10
00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:28.808
Salary in, a coffee, a book, the book refunded, fifty to a friend.

11
00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:31.363
Read the list, and the number makes sense.

12
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:33.420
So why can't the table say?

13
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.141
Watch how it got to 20.00.

14
00:00:35.141 --> 00:00:41.957
Every change is an UPDATE, a command that changes a row, and each one writes over the answer before it.

15
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:42.911
100.00.

16
00:00:42.911 --> 00:00:43.520
70.00.

17
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.130
25.00.

18
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:44.739
70.00.

19
00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:45.348
20.00.

20
00:00:45.348 --> 00:00:50.731
Each old number is gone the moment the new one lands.

21
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.398
You could bolt history back on, with a small program the database runs on every change, copying it into a second table.

22
00:00:58.398 --> 00:01:03.557
But now there are two records of the truth, and when they disagree, which one wins?

23
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.339
What we really want is the app's list as the truth itself.

24
00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:08.789
So turn it around.

25
00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:14.504
Store every change as an event, a record of one thing that happened, and never change it.

26
00:01:14.504 --> 00:01:20.091
The events go into a log you only add to the end of, like a notebook you never rub out.

27
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:25.984
Here's account acct-17's own list, its stream, each event with a version number.

28
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.522
Now the balance.

29
00:01:27.522 --> 00:01:35.096
Start from an empty account and apply each event in order, the way you'd add up a column one line at a time.

30
00:01:35.096 --> 00:01:37.059
What's the balance after v4?

31
00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:38.181
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:41.862
25.00.

33
00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:44.575
Zero, a hundred, seventy, twenty-five.

34
00:01:44.575 --> 00:01:49.859
Then the refund brings it back to seventy, and the transfer leaves twenty.

35
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.985
Adding up in order like that is called a fold.

36
00:01:52.985 --> 00:01:58.696
And the help line has its answer: a fifty transfer, after a card payment and its refund.

37
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.609
And questions the table never could answer are easy.

38
00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:06.508
The balance before the transfer?

39
00:02:06.508 --> 00:02:08.138
Fold to v5: 70.00.

40
00:02:08.138 --> 00:02:11.126
Everything that mentions pay-982?

41
00:02:11.126 --> 00:02:12.032
v4 and v5.

42
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.981
Keeping every change as an event, and working the state out from them, is called event sourcing.

43
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:26.359
The accounts table, if you keep one, is just a cache: a saved copy of where the log ends, that you can always work out again.

44
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.109
The table was never lying, by the way.

45
00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:31.221
It just had a very short memory.

46
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.701
Two words need to stay apart.

47
00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:38.191
A command is a request, an order: withdraw 80.00 from acct-17.

48
00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:39.495
It can be refused.

49
00:02:39.495 --> 00:02:43.189
Fold the log, the balance is 20.00, so this one is.

50
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:47.119
An event is a fact, in the past tense: 80.00 was withdrawn.

51
00:02:47.119 --> 00:02:50.427
It already happened, so nothing later may refuse it.

52
00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:53.861
Only a command that passes the rules becomes an event.

53
00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:56.617
And a mistake is never rubbed out.

54
00:02:56.617 --> 00:03:00.270
You add a correcting event, the way v5 refunds v4.

55
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.023
A bank books a reversal.

56
00:03:02.023 --> 00:03:05.019
It doesn't tear a page out of its ledger.

57
00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:08.463
Isn't this the change log from the first lesson?

58
00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:14.924
Change data capture reads row changes out of the database: the balance went from 100.00 to 70.00.

59
00:03:14.924 --> 00:03:17.255
That's only what the number became.

60
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:22.262
The event says MoneyWithdrawn, card pay-981: what happened, and why.

61
00:03:22.262 --> 00:03:26.604
So event sourcing means redesigning the program around events.

62
00:03:26.604 --> 00:03:28.635
You can't switch it on later.

63
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.885
And the log only grows, so folding years of events gets slow.

64
00:03:32.885 --> 00:03:35.889
Snapshots, later in this lesson, keep it fast.

65
00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:39.116
In Java and Kotlin the fold is a few lines.

66
00:03:39.116 --> 00:03:44.317
Start at zero, and for each event, add a deposit or take away a withdrawal.

67
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:48.233
So keep the list, and work the number out from it.

68
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:49.816
A balance tells you what.

69
00:03:49.816 --> 00:03:51.209
The log tells you why.

70
00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:52.896
One quick check.

71
00:03:52.896 --> 00:03:56.814
Support wants the balance as it was right after v3.

72
00:03:56.814 --> 00:03:58.658
Can the table tell them?

73
00:03:58.658 --> 00:03:59.580
Can the log?

74
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:00.809
Pause and think.

75
00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:05.398
The table can't.

76
00:04:05.398 --> 00:04:07.105
It only knows 20.00.

77
00:04:07.105 --> 00:04:10.517
The log can: fold to v3, and it's 70.00.
